## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Get Cool in the Shade – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Get Cool in the Shade.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following multi-step school procedures. Communication: Relay key points from the teacher's lectures to absent students.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist classmates in following conservation steps with visuals (e.g., shows a picture of a light switch and says, "Turn off the lights."). Can relay key points about conservation, using basic words and gestures (e.g., "We save water." and mimes turning off a faucet). Can summarize conservation actions for peers, using simple words and visuals (e.g., pointing to a tree picture and saying, "Save trees, don't waste paper.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of simple conservation actions (e.g., a light switch being turned off, a faucet with dripping water, a tree). Students call out "Save light!" or "Save water!" or "Save trees!" The teacher asks, "What can we do to make our school cool in the shade?" and encourages answers related to energy saving. The activity aims to activate prior knowledge about conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of someone turning off a light. The teacher says, "Look! We need to save energy. When you leave the classroom, turn off the lights. It's easy! This helps our environment." The teacher uses gestures (miming turning off a switch) and emphasizes the pronunciation of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off." The teacher then models how to assist a peer by turning to a student and saying, "Pedro, when we go outside, turn off the lights, okay? It saves energy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I see the lights on and no one is in the room, what should I do?" (Expected answer: "Turn off the lights.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I tell my friend 'Turn off the water,' am I helping them save water?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful poster titled "Our School's Conservation Rules!" displaying 3-4 simple conservation procedures using visuals and short imperative sentences (e.g., "Turn off the lights when you leave.", "Don't waste water.", "Recycle paper and plastic.", "Use natural light."). The poster also includes simple phrases like "It's easier to save energy now." and "Saving water is the most important."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Relaying key points from school procedures and using simple comparatives/superlatives to emphasize importance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a rule on the poster, e.g., "Don't waste water." Students repeat. The teacher asks, "What should we do with water?" (Gist question: "Save water.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads sentences from the poster: "Turn off the lights when you leave." and "Recycle paper and plastic." Then asks: "True or False: We should leave the lights on all the time?" (True/False question: False). "True or False: We should recycle paper?" (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to "Saving water is the most important" and asks, "What is super important to save?" (Expected answer: "Water.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Giving and following suggested actions using imperatives. Comparing actions using comparatives and superlatives. Vocabulary: shade, light, water, energy, recycle, turn off, save, conserve, cool, easy, important. Pronunciation: /sh/ in "shade", /t/ in "turn off". Phonemic awareness: -er for safer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Matching/Cloze):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of conservation actions and sentence frames. Students match the pictures to imperative phrases (e.g., "____ off the lights," "____ water") or fill in the blanks with target verbs (turn, save, recycle). The teacher guides students to pronounce /sh/ and /t/ clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Building):</b> In pairs, students receive sentence strips with parts of comparative and superlative sentences about conservation. For example, "Saving electricity is ____ (easy) than saving water." or "____ (important) thing to do is recycle." They complete the sentences using `easier` or `the most important`. The teacher models "It's easier to save energy wherever possible because it is beneficial and better for the environment."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Relay Race):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher whispers a simple conservation instruction (e.g., "Turn off the faucet when you brush your teeth.") to one student in each pair. This student then turns to their partner and, using simple words, gestures, and the imperative, relays the instruction. The partner then quickly writes down the action. The teacher circulates, checking for clear mediation and correct pronunciation of /t/ in "faucet."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Conservation Log Explanation Stations." Students will work in small groups of 3-4. Each student takes turns being the "Conservation Expert" for their "Daily Conservation Log" project. The student explains 2-3 entries from their log to their group members, acting as if they are assisting an absent classmate or explaining how to follow these steps at school. They use simple imperative sentences, gestures, and possibly drawings from their log.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students retrieve their "Daily Conservation Log" from the 21st Century Skills Project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In groups, one student (the "Expert") chooses 2-3 conservation actions from their log (e.g., "Turn off the lights to save energy.", "Recycle paper.", "Don't waste water.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Expert" explains these actions to their group members, using simple words and gestures, as if guiding them on how to save energy/water/etc. They can say, "Look at my log. First, turn off the lights. It's important to save electricity." They can also compare, "Saving electricity is easier than remembering to close the faucet completely."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group members listen and can ask simple clarifying questions (e.g., "What about water?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles until everyone has had a chance to be the "Conservation Expert."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors and provides support for language use and clarity, noting examples of good mediation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student selects one specific conservation action from their "Daily Conservation Log" project. In pairs, one student acts as the "Teacher" and explains their chosen action to their partner (the "Student"), using visuals (from their log or drawing quickly) and simple imperative sentences. The "Student" then uses a simple "Peer Assessment Checklist" to evaluate their partner's explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Peer Assessment Checklist (Simple):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner used action words (e.g., "Turn off"). (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I understood the conservation step. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner used a picture or gesture. (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My partner used /sh/ in "shade" or /t/ in "turn off" correctly. (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /sh/ and /t/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., light, water, save, turn off).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation using imperative sentences and simple words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of visual aids or gestures to assist understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active listening during peer assessment.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I explain how to save energy or water to a friend?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easier to explain today: turning off lights or recycling paper?" (Students write their answer in their notebooks.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining conservation steps to your classmates today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you be a better 'conservation helper' at school and at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate and explain conservation steps? The use of visual logs and simple imperatives was effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which specific vocabulary (e.g., electricity, faucet) or grammar (comparatives/superlatives in mediation) needs more support or practice for A1.3 level? Some students struggled with comparatives like "easier than."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the mediation tasks appropriately challenging for 5th graders at A1.3? Yes, assisting peers with simple actions using visuals was suitable.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Let's Get Cool in the Shade" theme. The next unit will build on collaborative communication skills by introducing a new theme and set of vocabulary, encouraging students to continue applying their ability to explain and assist peers in new contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>